  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to forecast the probability that by each of the deadlines (2026, 2030, etc.), there will be a complete solution to at least one of the Millennium Prize Problems, where AI contributed a new mathematical argument or proof step. Let me break this down.\n\nFirst, understanding the problem: The user is asking about the likelihood of these major unsolved math problems getting solved with AI's contribution. The key here is that AI must have contributed a new proof step. So, even if a problem is solved, if AI didn't contribute a critical step, it doesn't count. Got it.\n\nNow, what do I know about these problems? The Millennium Prize includes P vs NP, Riemann Hypothesis, Navier-Stokes, Hodge, Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, and Yang-Mills. These are all highly complex problems that have eluded even top mathematicians for decades. For example, Riemann Hypothesis is over 160 years old without proof. P vs NP is a foundational problem in computer science, still unsolved for 40+ years.\n\nWhat about AI's role in mathematics? There have been some recent advancements where AI helps discover new theorems or patterns. For example, DeepMind's work on the Collatz conjecture or certain knot theory problems. But these are more about generating conjectures or finding patterns, not necessarily providing rigorous proofs. A complete solution to a Millennium Problem would require not just pattern recognition but a full proof, which is much harder.\n\nNow, let's think about timelines. The forecasting date is 2025-04-29. The deadlines go up to 2050. The probability should be non-decreasing with each deadline, and account for the possibility that it might never happen. So, I need to assign a lower probability for 2026 and increase as the deadlines get further out, but not too high even in 2050 because some of these problems are extremely difficult.\n\nFor 2026 (just 1.5 years after the forecast date), the chances are probably very low. These are not the kind of problems that can be solved quickly, even with AI. You need a breakthrough idea, which takes time. Maybe a 5% chance here.\n\nBy 2030 (5 years later), maybe a few more projects have been initiated, and AI tools become more integrated into mathematical research. Possibly around 10-15%? Maybe 12%?\n\n2035 is 10 years from 2025. Suppose AI becomes more advanced, maybe we see collaborations between mathematicians and AI leading to partial results. But a complete solution is still difficult. Maybe 20% here.\n\n2040 is 15 years out. If by then AI systems are significantly better at creative problem-solving, perhaps handling proof construction or identifying new axioms, the chance could rise to 25-30%? Maybe 28%?\n\n2050 is 25 years later. Given enough time, maybe a revolution in AI and collaborative systems allows for such a breakthrough. But even so, considering the difficulty of the problems, perhaps 35-40%? Let's say 38%.\n\nBut wait, I need to check if my probabilities are increasing but not too much. The max here is under 40%, considering that these are extremely tough problems. Also, the requirement that AI must contribute a new proof step is another layer of difficulty. So, even if a solution is found by a human team, it might not count if AI didn't contribute a step. However, if humans and AI collaborate, then AI's contribution is verifiable. So, perhaps the overall probability is lower than standard problem-solving, but with the assumption that AI's role is growing.\n\nAnother angle: What's the likelihood of a solution to any one of these problems by these dates, without AI, and then adjust upwards if AI is a help. For example, the Riemann Hypothesis has had some recent computational checks but no proof. But if AI can find new patterns or shortcuts, maybe it could lead to a breakthrough. However, such breakthroughs are not routine.\n\nFor example, the Navier-Stokes problem is about existence and smoothness. This is a problem that might require a new mathematical framework. AI perhaps could model fluid dynamics in novel ways, but translating that into a proof is tough.\n\nConsidering all of this, my initial probability estimations for each deadline:\n\n2026: 0.03 (low due to short timeframe)\n2030: 0.08 (moderate progress but still not likely)\n2035: 0.15 (some chance with more advanced AI by then)\n2040: 0.25 (by then, maybe AI is more deeply integrated in research, leading to contributions)\n2050: 0.40 (after 25 years, a 40% probability given sustained AI advancements and collaborations)\n\nWait, but the user said \"nondecreasing with the deadline\". So each subsequent date should have equal or higher probability. The above is non-decreasing. However, I should make sure to not go over 0.5 or 0.6 because these are very hard problems. Maybe my initial estimates are a bit optimistic?\n\nAlternatively, let's think of it as cumulative. For example:\n\nBy 2026, almost negligible. Maybe 0.04.\n\n2030: Maybe 0.07.\n\n2035: 0.15.\n\n2040: 0.25.\n\n2050: 0.35.\n\nBut the user said \"nondecreasing\", so each must be >= previous. Also, the final probability is the upper bound. Considering that, perhaps start lower.\n\nAnother angle: Recent studies on problem-solving in mathematics with AI. For example, DeepMind's DeepMath project, or DeepMind's recent work in collaboration with mathematicians. They've had some successes in discovering conjectures (e.g., in knot theory), but proofs are still human-driven. So, for a full proof with AI contribution, maybe the progress is slower.\n\nTherefore, perhaps the probabilities are lower than my initial estimates. Maybe:\n\n2026: 0.02\n\n2030: 0.05\n\n2035: 0.10\n\n2040: 0.20\n\n2050: 0.30\n\nBut this might be too low. How about the following:\n\n2026: 0.03\n\n2030: 0.08\n\n2035: 0.15\n\n2040: 0.25\n\n2050: 0.35\n\nBut considering the difficulty and the role of AI, perhaps the probabilities are even lower. Let me adjust:\n\n2026: 0.02 (2%)\n\n2030: 0.05 (5%)\n\n2035: 0.10 (10%)\n\n2040: 0.15 (15%)\n\n2050: 0.25 (25%)\n\nThat's a possible progression, but even so, by 2050, 25% seems low. Alternatively, considering that AI might have a cumulative effect, the probabilities after 2040 could increase more. For example, maybe the probabilities are:\n\n2026: 0.03\n\n2030: 0.07\n\n2035: 0.15\n\n2040: 0.25\n\n2050: 0.40\n\nThis seems plausible. Each time, the probability increases by 3-5 points until it reaches 40% by 2050. The 2026 has a very low chance, given the time. By 2050, perhaps AI has become a standard part of research, and there's a reasonable chance one of these age-old problems is solved with AI's help.\n\nBut another consideration is that some of the problems (like Riemann Hypothesis) might require entirely new mathematical frameworks, which are unpredictable. AI might not be able to contribute just steps but entire new theories. That's a harder nut to crack.\n\nIn conclusion, based on these considerations, I'll go with the probabilities increasing gradually, peaking at 0.4 by 2050. So:\n\n\"2026\": 0.03,\n\n\"2030\": 0.07,\n\n\"2035\": 0.15,\n\n\"2040\": 0.28,\n\n\"2050\": 0.40.\n\nThis seems reasonable. Each deadline offers a higher chance as time goes on, but even by 2050, the probability isn't very high due to the problem's difficulty.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.03, \"2030\": 0.07, \"2035\": 0.15, \"2040\": 0.25, \"2050\": 0.38}",
